diff --git a/HAPTICS.md b/HAPTICS.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3d62189
--- /dev/null
+++ b/HAPTICS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,124 @@
+# Pressure haptics extension
+
+This extension adds a **Haptics** group to the **Pointer** tab of Wellspring
+Control Center (next to Force Touch). It connects real pressure measurements
+from Wellspring to a separate Windows KMDF actuator driver, so a firm press
+after the physical click fires a second haptic click.
+
+**Status:** the actuator protocol was exercised on a 2019 16-inch MacBook Pro
+(`MacBookPro16,1`, USB `05AC:0340`) by the prototype driver this one replaces.
+`AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm` itself has been compile-checked but **not yet run on
+hardware**. Other models are not enabled by the INF.
+
+## Components
+
+- Control Center (`src/AmtPtpConfigGui`): the settings, and the engine that
+  does the work (`HapticsEngine.cs`). No separate program is involved.
+- `src/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm`: the actuator driver, a small KMDF function driver
+  for one interface (`MI_03`) of the trackpad's composite USB device. It is
+  independent of the touchpad driver: the touchpad driver filters `MI_02`, this
+  one owns `MI_03`, and neither knows about the other. Its only job is to play a
+  fixed click waveform on request.
+
+## Settings
+
+Haptic settings are part of the **profile**, like Palm, Pointer and Scroll:
+they follow profile switching, **Reset to defaults**, profile export/import and
+backup/restore, and they are applied by the bottom-bar **Save**. A profile or
+file written before haptics existed keeps the current haptic settings on load.
+The group is shown only on trackpads with a pressure sensor, like Force Touch.
+
+## Behavior
+
+A background thread inside Control Center reads Wellspring's live pressure
+frames and fires one click through the actuator driver. It keeps working while
+the window is hidden in the tray, but not after the app is closed (use
+"Start with Windows" to have it available after sign-in). While haptics are off,
+or the actuator driver is not installed, it asks the driver for nothing.
+
+The default threshold is 265 raw pressure units, with release at 70% of that
+value. Two fresh samples above threshold trigger one waveform. Holding the
+press does not repeat it; pressure must fall below the release threshold to
+rearm. It also requires one confident non-palm contact and physical
+button-down, imposes a 250 ms cooldown, and resets after a stale feed.
+
+The raw scale is device-specific; use the live meter to pick a threshold.
+Light/firm are captured waveform names, not a guarantee of perceptibly
+different strength on every device. **Test click** plays the selected waveform
+without pressing, saved or not.
+
+Force Touch's mouse action should be disabled ("Force Touch action" in the
+Pointer tab) when only haptic feedback is wanted. This extension does not
+change pointer settings automatically.
+
+The Live view and haptics share the driver's live stream (`DeviceIo.SetLiveConsumer`):
+it stays on while either wants it, so hiding the window to the tray or turning
+Live off does not stop haptics. The engine recovers on its own after the driver
+restarts, sleep/wake, or the actuator reappearing.
+
+## Actuator driver
+
+The driver is organised like the touchpad driver (`Driver`, `Device`, `Queue`,
+`Trace`, `Public`) plus `Actuator.c`, which holds everything hardware-specific.
+
+- **Interface contract** (`Public.h`, mirrored in `HapticsActuator.cs`): a device
+  interface GUID and two IOCTLs, `GET_INFO` and `CLICK`, with versioned
+  structures. Control Center checks the driver's interface version when it opens
+  the device and refuses a driver built from a different revision.
+- **Identification**: it sends nothing to a device it has not positively
+  identified (supported vendor/product, exactly one interface, interface number
+  3, HID class). Supported products are one table in `Actuator.c`; the INF lists
+  the same IDs. Only `05AC:0340` (MacBookPro16,1) is enabled - add a model only
+  after confirming it exposes the actuator on `MI_03` and accepts these reports.
+- **Click path**: the click is an asynchronous HID SET_REPORT on a request and
+  buffer allocated once, so a click allocates nothing and cannot fail for lack of
+  memory. Clicks closer than 100 ms are refused with `STATUS_DEVICE_BUSY`
+  (flow control: the actuator is not a toy to hammer).
+- **Power and removal**: no idle power management (the first click after an idle
+  suspend would pay resume latency). `EvtIoStop` cancels an in-flight transfer on
+  removal and lets it finish across a suspend.
+- **Access**: opens are checked against the SDDL set in `Device.c`: administrators
+  and SYSTEM full access, other signed-in users read/write. That lets an
+  unelevated Control Center fire a click, and no more than that.
+- **Diagnostics**: `GET_INFO` reports delivered/throttled/failed click counters
+  and the last transfer status. Set `DebugMode` (REG_DWORD, 1) under the device's
+  `Device Parameters` key to get `[AmtHaptic] ...` lines in DebugView.
+
+The waveform payloads are the actuator's own protocol; they follow the t2linux
+project's `t2_trackpad_actuator` captures (GPL-2.0, like this project). Do not
+edit a byte without testing it on the hardware.
+
+## Build and validation
+
+Control Center builds exactly as before; there is nothing extra to publish or
+install for the GUI side. The actuator driver builds with the same toolchain as
+the touchpad driver (`AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm.vcxproj`, same configuration matrix).
+Like the touchpad driver, its INF is not part of the project: CI stamps it
+(StampInf), catalogs it (Inf2Cat) and signs it. `BuildAll` ships it in a separate
+`HapticDriver` folder of the release archives (not in the installer), and
+`StaticAnalysis` runs the same strict PREfast pass over it. Driver signing and
+installation are otherwise deliberately separate. Do not distribute a local
+private signing key, certificate trust setup, or machine-specific driver backup
+with source.
+
+## Experimental driver deployment
+
+Current locally built drivers require appropriate signing/Test Mode. Installing
+or testing kernel drivers can crash Windows or disrupt input. Keep a keyboard
+or external mouse available and export the existing pointer package first.
+Do not disable Test Mode while dependent experimental drivers are installed.
+Revert each affected interface to its original driver before cleaning up test
+trust. Never replace the composite parent, keyboard, or Apple USB host driver.
+
+Sleep/wake recovery and long-term stability have not been established. If the
+pressure feed disconnects, the helper stops; the panel then offers Start helper.
+
+## Attribution
+
+WellspringPTP is by Osaul4ik and derives from imbushuo/mac-precision-touchpad.
+The captured actuator waveforms and protocol are adapted from André Eikmeyer's
+`kaiT2en/KaiT2en-Fedora`, specifically
+`modules/t2_trackpad_actuator/t2_trackpad_actuator.c` (GPL-2.0-only):
+https://github.com/kaiT2en/KaiT2en-Fedora
+
+The new actuator/helper source is GPL-2.0-only; see `LICENSE-GPL.md`.
diff --git a/src/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm/Actuator.c b/src/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm/Actuator.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a3b6cb1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm/Actuator.c
@@ -0,0 +1,234 @@
+// Actuator.c - How a click is encoded and sent to the actuator.
+//
+// WHY THE REQUEST IS PREALLOCATED AND REUSED
+// A click must not fail because the system is short of memory at the wrong
+// moment, and it should not allocate on every press. AmtHapticAllocateTransfer
+// therefore creates one request and one report buffer when the device is
+// created; every click re-formats the same request (WdfRequestReuse) and
+// copies its waveform into the same buffer. This is safe because the sequential
+// default queue (Queue.c) never has two clicks in flight, and the client's
+// request is only completed at the very end of the completion routine, after
+// the request has been made reusable - so the next click cannot start earlier.
+//
+// WHY THE TRANSFER IS ASYNCHRONOUS
+// The caller's thread only waits for the click's own completion, but no
+// framework thread is parked inside the driver while the USB transfer runs, and
+// the transfer can be cancelled (AmtHapticCancelClick) when the device powers
+// down or is removed.
+
+#include "Driver.h"
+
+#ifdef ALLOC_PRAGMA
+#pragma alloc_text (PAGE, AmtHapticAllocateTransfer)
+#endif
+
+// Captured actuator waveforms (HID output report 0x53, 14 bytes). The bytes
+// are the actuator's own protocol, not something this driver can derive; they
+// follow the t2linux project's t2_trackpad_actuator payloads. Do not edit a
+// value without testing it on the hardware.
+static const UCHAR AmtHapticReports[AMT_HAPTIC_WAVEFORM_COUNT][AMT_HAPTIC_REPORT_LENGTH] =
+{
+    // AMT_HAPTIC_WAVEFORM_LIGHT
+    { 0x53, 0x01, 0x1C, 0x8F, 0x01, 0x03, 0x00, 0x53, 0x37, 0x02, 0x02, 0x00, 0x18, 0x4D },
+
+    // AMT_HAPTIC_WAVEFORM_FIRM
+    { 0x53, 0x01, 0x2D, 0x8F, 0x01, 0x05, 0x00, 0x53, 0x37, 0x02, 0x02, 0x00, 0x30, 0x4D },
+};
+
+// Product IDs of the trackpad's composite USB device on which the actuator is
+// known to be interface 3. Add one only after confirming that model really
+// exposes the actuator there and accepts these reports, and add the matching
+// hardware ID to the INF.
+static const USHORT AmtHapticSupportedProductIds[] =
+{
+    0x0340, // MacBookPro16,1 (2019 16-inch)
+};
+
+static EVT_WDF_REQUEST_COMPLETION_ROUTINE AmtHapticEvtTransferComplete;
+
+BOOLEAN
+AmtHapticIsSupportedDevice(
+    _In_ USHORT VendorId,
+    _In_ USHORT ProductId
+)
+{
+    ULONG i;
+
+    if (VendorId != AMT_HAPTIC_VENDOR_ID) {
+        return FALSE;
+    }
+
+    for (i = 0; i < RTL_NUMBER_OF(AmtHapticSupportedProductIds); i++) {
+        if (AmtHapticSupportedProductIds[i] == ProductId) {
+            return TRUE;
+        }
+    }
+
+    return FALSE;
+}
+
+_IRQL_requires_(PASSIVE_LEVEL)
+NTSTATUS
+AmtHapticAllocateTransfer(
+    _In_ WDFDEVICE Device
+)
+{
+    PDEVICE_CONTEXT       context = DeviceGetContext(Device);
+    WDF_OBJECT_ATTRIBUTES attributes;
+    NTSTATUS              status;
+
+    PAGED_CODE();
+
+    // Both objects are parented to the device, so the framework releases them
+    // with it; there is no cleanup callback to write.
+    WDF_OBJECT_ATTRIBUTES_INIT(&attributes);
+    attributes.ParentObject = Device;
+
+    status = WdfRequestCreate(&attributes, NULL, &context->TransferRequest);
+    if (!NT_SUCCESS(status)) {
+        return status;
+    }
+
+    WDF_OBJECT_ATTRIBUTES_INIT(&attributes);
+    attributes.ParentObject = Device;
+
+    return WdfMemoryCreate(
+        &attributes,
+        NonPagedPoolNx,
+        AMT_HAPTIC_POOL_TAG,
+        AMT_HAPTIC_REPORT_LENGTH,
+        &context->ReportMemory,
+        NULL);
+}
+
+NTSTATUS
+AmtHapticStartClick(
+    _In_ PDEVICE_CONTEXT DeviceContext,
+    _In_ WDFREQUEST      Request,
+    _In_ ULONG           Waveform
+)
+{
+    WDF_USB_CONTROL_SETUP_PACKET setup;
+    WDF_REQUEST_SEND_OPTIONS     options;
+    WDF_REQUEST_REUSE_PARAMS     reuse;
+    PVOID                        buffer;
+    NTSTATUS                     status;
+
+    if (Waveform >= AMT_HAPTIC_WAVEFORM_COUNT) {
+        return STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER;
+    }
+
+    if (DeviceContext->TransferRequestNeedsReuse) {
+        WDF_REQUEST_REUSE_PARAMS_INIT(&reuse, WDF_REQUEST_REUSE_NO_FLAGS, STATUS_SUCCESS);
+        status = WdfRequestReuse(DeviceContext->TransferRequest, &reuse);
+        if (!NT_SUCCESS(status)) {
+            return status;
+        }
+        DeviceContext->TransferRequestNeedsReuse = FALSE;
+    }
+
+    buffer = WdfMemoryGetBuffer(DeviceContext->ReportMemory, NULL);
+    RtlCopyMemory(buffer, AmtHapticReports[Waveform], AMT_HAPTIC_REPORT_LENGTH);
+
+    // HID class SET_REPORT(Output, report ID), addressed to the interface the
+    // device was validated to be (never a hard-coded number here).
+    WDF_USB_CONTROL_SETUP_PACKET_INIT_CLASS(
+        &setup,
+        BmRequestHostToDevice,
+        BmRequestToInterface,
+        AMT_HID_REQUEST_SET_REPORT,
+        AMT_HID_REPORT_VALUE(AMT_HID_REPORT_TYPE_OUTPUT, AMT_HAPTIC_REPORT_ID),
+        DeviceContext->InterfaceNumber);
+
+    status = WdfUsbTargetDeviceFormatRequestForControlTransfer(
+        DeviceContext->UsbDevice,
+        DeviceContext->TransferRequest,
+        &setup,
+        DeviceContext->ReportMemory,
+        NULL);
+
+    if (!NT_SUCCESS(status)) {
+        DeviceContext->TransferRequestNeedsReuse = TRUE;
+        return status;
+    }
+
+    WdfRequestSetCompletionRoutine(
+        DeviceContext->TransferRequest,
+        AmtHapticEvtTransferComplete,
+        Request);
+
+    WDF_REQUEST_SEND_OPTIONS_INIT(&options, WDF_REQUEST_SEND_OPTION_TIMEOUT);
+    WDF_REQUEST_SEND_OPTIONS_SET_TIMEOUT(&options, WDF_REL_TIMEOUT_IN_MS(AMT_HAPTIC_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT_MS));
+
+    if (!WdfRequestSend(
+            DeviceContext->TransferRequest,
+            WdfUsbTargetDeviceGetIoTarget(DeviceContext->UsbDevice),
+            &options)) {
+
+        // The completion routine is not called when the send itself fails, so
+        // the request is reset here, lazily, before its next use.
+        status = WdfRequestGetStatus(DeviceContext->TransferRequest);
+        DeviceContext->TransferRequestNeedsReuse = TRUE;
+
+        return NT_SUCCESS(status) ? STATUS_UNSUCCESSFUL : status;
+    }
+
+    // From here on the completion routine owns Request; do not touch it.
+    return STATUS_PENDING;
+}
+
+VOID
+AmtHapticCancelClick(
+    _In_ PDEVICE_CONTEXT DeviceContext
+)
+{
+    (VOID)WdfRequestCancelSentRequest(DeviceContext->TransferRequest);
+}
+
+static
+VOID
+AmtHapticEvtTransferComplete(
+    _In_ WDFREQUEST                     TransferRequest,
+    _In_ WDFIOTARGET                    Target,
+    _In_ PWDF_REQUEST_COMPLETION_PARAMS Params,
+    _In_ WDFCONTEXT                     Context
+)
+// Runs when the control transfer finishes, fails, times out, or is cancelled.
+// IRQL <= DISPATCH_LEVEL.
+{
+    WDFREQUEST                          clientRequest = (WDFREQUEST)Context;
+    PDEVICE_CONTEXT                     deviceContext = DeviceGetContext(WdfIoTargetGetDevice(Target));
+    PWDF_USB_REQUEST_COMPLETION_PARAMS  usbParams = Params->Parameters.Usb.Completion;
+    WDF_REQUEST_REUSE_PARAMS            reuse;
+    NTSTATUS                            status = Params->IoStatus.Status;
+
+    // The transferred length comes from the USB-specific completion
+    // parameters of a control transfer, the one place that reports it
+    // reliably for a request formatted by WdfUsbTargetDeviceFormatRequestFor-
+    // ControlTransfer. A short transfer means the device took only part of the
+    // report: not a click that was felt.
+    if (NT_SUCCESS(status) &&
+        usbParams != NULL &&
+        usbParams->Type == WdfUsbRequestTypeDeviceControlTransfer &&
+        usbParams->Parameters.DeviceControlTransfer.Length != AMT_HAPTIC_REPORT_LENGTH) {
+
+        status = STATUS_DEVICE_DATA_ERROR;
+    }
+
+    InterlockedExchange(&deviceContext->LastStatus, status);
+    if (NT_SUCCESS(status)) {
+        InterlockedIncrement(&deviceContext->ClicksDelivered);
+    } else {
+        InterlockedIncrement(&deviceContext->ClicksFailed);
+    }
+
+    AmtHapticTrace(deviceContext, "click transfer finished, status=0x%08X\n", status);
+
+    // Make the request reusable BEFORE completing the client's request: the
+    // sequential queue starts the next click only after that completion, so no
+    // other thread can be using the request while it is reset here.
+    WDF_REQUEST_REUSE_PARAMS_INIT(&reuse, WDF_REQUEST_REUSE_NO_FLAGS, STATUS_SUCCESS);
+    deviceContext->TransferRequestNeedsReuse = !NT_SUCCESS(WdfRequestReuse(TransferRequest, &reuse));
+
+    WdfRequestComplete(clientRequest, status);
+}
diff --git a/src/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm/Actuator.h b/src/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm/Actuator.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1eeba6c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm/Actuator.h
@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+// Actuator.h - Hardware knowledge: which devices are supported and how a
+// click is encoded and sent.
+
+#pragma once
+
+EXTERN_C_START
+
+#define AMT_HAPTIC_POOL_TAG 'tpHA'
+
+// The actuator is a HID output report on one interface of the trackpad's
+// composite USB device. The driver binds to that interface only, never to the
+// composite parent and never to the interface the touchpad driver owns.
+#define AMT_HAPTIC_VENDOR_ID        0x05AC
+#define AMT_HAPTIC_INTERFACE_NUMBER 3
+
+// HID class SET_REPORT(Output, ID 0x53). Every payload is this long.
+#define AMT_HAPTIC_REPORT_ID        0x53
+#define AMT_HAPTIC_REPORT_LENGTH    14
+
+// HID class request SET_REPORT and the wValue it takes for an output report:
+// report type in the high byte, report ID in the low byte.
+#define AMT_HID_REQUEST_SET_REPORT       0x09
+#define AMT_HID_REPORT_TYPE_OUTPUT       0x02
+#define AMT_HID_REPORT_VALUE(Type, Id)   ((USHORT)(((Type) << 8) | (Id)))
+
+// Actuator wear/heat guard: clicks closer together than this are refused.
+// KeQueryInterruptTime() units are 100 ns.
+#define AMT_HAPTIC_MIN_CLICK_INTERVAL_MS   100
+#define AMT_HAPTIC_MIN_CLICK_INTERVAL_100NS \
+    ((ULONGLONG)AMT_HAPTIC_MIN_CLICK_INTERVAL_MS * 10000ULL)
+
+// A click is one tiny control transfer; anything slower than this is a fault.
+#define AMT_HAPTIC_TRANSFER_TIMEOUT_MS     500
+
+// TRUE for a USB device this driver is known to work on.
+BOOLEAN
+AmtHapticIsSupportedDevice(
+    _In_ USHORT VendorId,
+    _In_ USHORT ProductId
+);
+
+// Creates the request and report buffer reused for every click, so the click
+// path itself allocates nothing and cannot fail for lack of memory.
+_IRQL_requires_(PASSIVE_LEVEL)
+NTSTATUS
+AmtHapticAllocateTransfer(
+    _In_ WDFDEVICE Device
+);
+
+// Sends one click for Waveform and completes Request from the transfer's
+// completion routine. Returns STATUS_PENDING when that is under way; any other
+// status means nothing was sent and the CALLER must complete Request.
+NTSTATUS
+AmtHapticStartClick(
+    _In_ PDEVICE_CONTEXT DeviceContext,
+    _In_ WDFREQUEST      Request,
+    _In_ ULONG           Waveform
+);
+
+// Cancels a click whose transfer is still in flight; its completion routine
+// then completes the client's request. Harmless if none is.
+VOID
+AmtHapticCancelClick(
+    _In_ PDEVICE_CONTEXT DeviceContext
+);
+
+EXTERN_C_END
diff --git a/src/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm.inf b/src/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm.inf
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..09b0e8e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm/AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm.inf
@@ -0,0 +1,77 @@
+; DriverVer below is a placeholder - the CI workflow rewrites this line
+; automatically at build time (StampInf), together with $ARCH$ and
+; $KMDFVERSION$.
+;
+; Haptic actuator function driver for one interface of the Apple T2 trackpad's
+; composite USB device. It is NOT part of the touchpad driver and never binds to
+; the composite parent or to the touchpad's own interface (MI_02).
+
+[Version]
+Signature   = "$WINDOWS NT$"
+Class       = USBDevice
+ClassGuid   = {88BAE032-5A81-49f0-BC3D-A4FF138216D6}
+Provider    = %ManufacturerName%
+CatalogFile = AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm.cat
+DriverVer   = 01/01/2026,1.0.0.0
+PnpLockdown = 1
+
+[DestinationDirs]
+DefaultDestDir = 13
+AmtHaptic_Drivers_Dir = 13
+
+[SourceDisksNames]
+1 = %DiskName%,,,""
+
+[SourceDisksFiles]
+AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm.sys = 1,,
+
+[Manufacturer]
+%ManufacturerName% = Standard,NT$ARCH$
+
+[Standard.NT$ARCH$]
+; Actuator interface (MI_03) of the T2 trackpad. One line per model that is
+; CONFIRMED to expose the actuator there; AmtHapticIsSupportedDevice() in
+; Actuator.c must list the same product IDs.
+%AmtHaptic.DeviceDesc% = AmtHaptic_Device, USB\Vid_05ac&Pid_0340&MI_03
+
+[AmtHaptic_Drivers_Dir]
+AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm.sys
+
+; -------------- AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm device install --------------
+
+[AmtHaptic_Device.NT]
+CopyFiles = AmtHaptic_Drivers_Dir
+
+[AmtHaptic_Device.NT.HW]
+AddReg = AmtHaptic_AddReg
+
+[AmtHaptic_AddReg]
+; Runtime debug-trace switch (see Trace.h/Trace.c). 0 = off (shipped default).
+; Flip to 1 (regedit, this device's "Device Parameters" key) to enable
+; "[AmtHaptic] ..." DbgPrintEx output.
+HKR,,"DebugMode",0x00010003,0
+
+[AmtHaptic_Device.NT.Services]
+; This is the function driver of the interface, so it carries
+; SPSVCINST_ASSOCSERVICE (unlike the touchpad driver, which is a lower filter).
+AddService = AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm, %SPSVCINST_ASSOCSERVICE%, AmtHaptic_Service_Inst
+
+[AmtHaptic_Service_Inst]
+DisplayName   = %AmtHaptic.SVCDESC%
+ServiceType   = 1               ; SERVICE_KERNEL_DRIVER
+StartType     = 3               ; SERVICE_DEMAND_START
+ErrorControl  = 1               ; SERVICE_ERROR_NORMAL
+ServiceBinary = %13%\AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm.sys
+
+[AmtHaptic_Device.NT.Wdf]
+KmdfService = AmtHapticDeviceUsbKm, AmtHaptic_wdfsect
+
+[AmtHaptic_wdfsect]
+KmdfLibraryVersion = $KMDFVERSION$
+
+[Strings]
+SPSVCINST_ASSOCSERVICE  = 0x00000002
+ManufacturerName        = "Osaul4ik"
+DiskName                = "WellspringPTP Installation Disk"
+AmtHaptic.DeviceDesc    = "Wellspring Haptic Actuator"
+AmtHaptic.SVCDESC       = "Wellspring Haptic Actuator Service"
